+# Decision: Delivering committed changes to browsers
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event and assigns every committed edit a single event revision. Editors submit commands over HTTPS, but the path by which a committed revision reaches the up to 800 browsers watching a board is a separate choice. The board must show committed changes in connected browsers within 15 seconds under normal connectivity, survive opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second, and let a browser recover the latest board after up to 20 minutes offline.
+
+The choice is load-bearing because the change store, the delivery layer, and browser recovery are all sized around it: it determines whether the store serves cursor reads or publishes a replayable revision stream, whether delivery is stateless request/response or holds live sessions, and whether browsers poll, subscribe over a socket, or use a provider's subscription SDK. Getting it wrong means rebuilding three components, not one.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gate eliminates any option. All three can be built to meet the 15-second freshness floor, the 800-browser scale, and the 20-minute recovery window — these are background requirements every option satisfies, not differentiators. The tenet that event updates carry stall IDs and operational statuses, not customer records, means data sensitivity does not bar a managed provider either. The decision therefore turns entirely on weighted criteria.
+
+- **Vendor independence** (major) — freedom from dependence on an external realtime provider's availability, pricing, and roadmap. The board should not be tied to a vendor it cannot replace without rework.
+- **Update latency beyond the floor** (moderate) — latency lower than the 15-second requirement. Faster updates are useful but not required; subsecond is not a goal.
+- **Recurring operating cost** (moderate) — ongoing spend attributable to the delivery path beyond the infrastructure the project already runs.
+- **Operational burden** (moderate) — the connection capacity, session state, and reconnect/catch-up logic the team must build and operate.
+- **Failure visibility** (moderate; turns on the tenet *connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board*) — how readily a broken delivery path is distinguished from a board that genuinely has not changed.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers re-request the board every five seconds over ordinary HTTPS, passing a cursor for the last revision they hold; the server returns any revisions since. Delivery stays stateless request/response, the change store answers cursor queries, and browsers run a polling loop with no persistent connection.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor independence: **strong** — nothing beyond the project's own HTTPS stack.
+- Update latency beyond the floor: **weak** — updates lag by up to the poll interval, a few seconds; within the floor but the slowest of the three.
+- Recurring operating cost: **strong** — no per-connection or vendor cost; cost is repeated reads against the store.
+- Operational burden: **strong** — stateless, no session or connection state to manage; the simplest to operate.
+- Failure visibility: **strong** — each poll succeeds or fails on its own, so staleness is naturally bounded by the interval and a failed request is an explicit signal.
+
+**Summary.** Simplest and cheapest to operate, at the cost of the slowest updates and steady repeated reads.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** The server holds a persistent WebSocket connection to each browser and pushes new revisions as they commit, with no third-party realtime service. The change store retains replayable revisions and publishes them reliably to the delivery layer; delivery manages socket sessions and fans committed revisions out to subscribed browsers; browsers hold a subscription, detect a dropped connection, and catch up from their last-seen revision on reconnect.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor independence: **strong** — delivery runs entirely on project-owned infrastructure.
+- Update latency beyond the floor: **strong** — revisions are pushed on commit, well inside the floor.
+- Recurring operating cost: **strong** — no vendor bill; cost is the project's own connection-handling infrastructure.
+- Operational burden: **weak** — the project owns connection capacity for 800 concurrent sockets, opening-time bursts, and the reconnect/catch-up path; the heaviest of the three to build and run.
+- Failure visibility: **partial** — a silently dropped socket can resemble an unchanged board unless reconnect detection is built explicitly; achievable, but it is work the option must carry to satisfy the failure-visibility tenet.
+
+**Summary.** Fast, vendor-free updates paid for with the most connection and reconnect operations work.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A managed realtime provider carries revisions to browsers. The change store reconciles its publications against the provider, delivery wraps the provider's publish API and manages credentials, and browsers use the provider's subscription SDK.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor independence: **weak** — the board depends on the provider's availability, pricing, and subscription model, and cannot be replaced without rework in all three components.
+- Update latency beyond the floor: **strong** — low-latency push is the provider's core function.
+- Recurring operating cost: **weak** — recurring per-connection or per-message fees that scale with 800 browsers and burst traffic.
+- Operational burden: **partial** — the provider absorbs connection scaling and reconnect handling, but the project takes on publication reconciliation and credential management.
+- Failure visibility: **partial** — depends on the provider's connection-state signals, which the browser must surface rather than treat as an unchanged board.
+
+**Summary.** The least connection-operations work and strong latency, bought with recurring fees and coupling to a provider.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.**
+
+The decision turns on **vendor independence**, the single major criterion. Both polling and self-hosted sockets are strong on it; the hosted realtime service is weak, and that weakness — the board coupled to a provider's availability, pricing, and roadmap across all three components — is what removes it from contention despite its strong latency and low operational burden. That narrows the choice to the two self-owned options.
+
+Between them, **update latency** breaks the tie. Polling meets the 15-second floor but is the weakest option on latency, lagging by the poll interval and generating steady repeated reads; self-hosted sockets push on commit. The useful-but-not-required latency improvement is worth claiming here because it comes without surrendering vendor independence or taking on recurring cost — both options are strong on those.
+
+The price is **operational burden**, where self-hosted sockets are the weakest of all three: the project owns connection capacity for 800 concurrent browsers and opening-time bursts, plus the reconnect and catch-up path. That burden is accepted deliberately in exchange for fast updates with no realtime-vendor dependency. The **failure-visibility** tenet shapes that burden rather than opposing the choice — it is why the reconnect path must explicitly distinguish a dropped connection from an unchanged board, work the delivery and browser-sync nodes now own.
+
+This choice is not forced by a tenet: *self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted* leaves the hosted service admissible, so the selection is a deliberate lean to vendor independence over minimum operational burden, neither of which the HLD had named as overriding.
+
+**Implications.** Committing forecloses the stateless simplicity of polling and the managed connection-scaling of a hosted service. Downstream it obligates: a change store that retains replayable revisions and publishes them reliably to delivery; a delivery layer that owns socket sessions and fan-out; and a browser-sync layer that subscribes, detects disconnection, and catches up from its last-seen revision within the 20-minute recovery window.
