 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for committed-change delivery.** The server pushes committed revisions to browsers over WebSocket connections it hosts itself. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected for its repeated reads and seconds of lag; a hosted realtime service was rejected for recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosting accepts the operational burden of owning connection capacity and reconnect handling in exchange for fast updates with no realtime-vendor dependency. See `docs/decisions/change-delivery.md`.
